+From: EglantinePS5 <Vampinity@users.noreply.github.com>
+Subject: up: HLE reply buffers kept per service thread, and a refused reply set-up retried once (EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER, EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY), off by default
+
+What: both warm first-start runs of 12 Nov (run1234, run1236, T130) ended a second after the house
+door opened, with std::bad_alloc on fsp-srv (the one file-service thread) in the reply wrapper's
+output buffer for one IStorage::Read (902,912 and 480,902 bytes): the heap had about 0.9 GiB free in
+pieces and could not grow. CmifReplyWrapImpl (cmif_serialization.h) makes three fresh ScratchBuffers
+for every request, so every request with an output buffer is one new[] of the game's buffer size.
+With EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER=1 each service thread keeps three thread-local buffers and swaps them
+into the wrapper's own array for a request (a pointer swap, no copy), ReadInArgument and
+WriteOutArgument run unchanged on them, and the lease's destructor swaps them back after the reply
+is written (also on an exception). A kept buffer grows to the largest request its thread has seen
+and never shrinks; a request over the cap, or one that comes while the thread's buffers are in use
+further up the same thread (a core-3 service fiber), gets a one-off buffer as before. Only slots
+that are real output buffers are touched (compile-time OutBufferSlotMask); a command with no output
+buffer gets no lease. With EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY=1 a std::bad_alloc in the wrapper's set-up only
+(the lease's Take and ReadInArgument, before the service's code runs and before the reply is
+written into the command buffer) is caught: the buffers that were to grow are freed, the heap
+trims (eden_heap_trim), the thread counts as a waiting request (eden_heap_want, front part:
+headless/heap_arenas.inc, so the caches see memory short and the texture collector evicts at its
+hardest) for the pause, and the set-up runs once more; refused again, the same exception is thrown
+on (the session ends as before). A refusal inside the service's own code is never caught.
+
+Switches (read once, at the first reply-wrapper request): EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER=1 (exact "1");
+EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER_CAP_MIB (1-64, unset 4); EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY=1 (exact "1");
+EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY_MS (0-2000, unset 200). Test aid, never in a play env:
+EDEN_TEST_HLE_ALLOC_FAIL=<n>[,<after_s>[,<service>]]: from <after_s> s after the read, the n-th
+output-buffer set-up that is about to allocate throws std::bad_alloc, once; <service> (its
+GetServiceName(), IStorage for the door's file read) limits the count to that service.
+Log (stderr, snprintf + fputs, no heap use): at the read "EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER each service
+thread keeps its reply buffers (up to 4 MiB each) instead of a new one per request", "EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY
+a refused reply set-up trims the heap, waits 200 ms with memory wanted, and runs once more",
+"EDEN_TEST_HLE_ALLOC_FAIL reply buffer allocation number N, counted from S s on (service X),
+throws std::bad_alloc (once)"; first act "EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER acts: a reply of N bytes built
+in a kept buffer of M, no allocation"; first over-cap "... is over the 4 MiB cap: one-off buffer as
+before"; per caught refusal "EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY <service> command <n> was refused memory before
+it ran (output buffers a/b/c bytes): the heap gave back X MiB, waiting N ms, then once more", then
+"EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY served on the second try" or "... refused again: failing as before"; per
+test throw "EDEN_TEST_HLE_ALLOC_FAIL throws std::bad_alloc for a reply buffer of N bytes"; every
+10 s from a request while any is on "EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER buffers= allocations_saved=
+allocated= grew= over_cap= nested= held_kib= threads= largest=" (or "EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER off:
+buffers= allocated= largest="), "| EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY caught= served= failed= gave_back_mib=",
+"| EDEN_TEST_HLE_ALLOC_FAIL counted= thrown=".
+
+Not in TranslationSwitches (no shader change).
+
+Cost when off: one check of a function-local static per reply-wrapper request (HleReply::Get().any)
+and one empty std::optional made and dropped for a command with an output buffer; ReadInArgument
+runs in an inlined lambda; no thread-local touched, no line, no try block (a refusal is thrown from
+where it is thrown today). The front function is never called unless the retry is on.
+
+Default flip (later, after the test-aid arms and the real door): core/hle/service/hle_reply_buffers.h
+ReadConfig(): "config.reuse = ExactOne("EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER");" becomes on unless set to 0,
+the same for "config.retry = ExactOne("EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY");". One line each.
+
+Risks: with reuse, bytes a service did not write reach the game as the previous reply's bytes on
+that thread instead of fresh-heap leftovers (both undefined; not the real console's behaviour
+either); kept buffers are held for good (per thread up to 3 x cap, held_kib); on a core-3 service
+fiber the retry's pause blocks every game thread on core 3; the retry may move the failure to the
+graphics side, which was also out of memory at that door. Unwinding from the PS5 operator new
+through the wrapper is unproven on the console (the real-door arm is the proof).
+
+Origin and credit: our own. Design precedent only (no code taken): the console's service framework
+as rebuilt by Atmosphere (libstratosphere) keeps one pointer buffer per session. Eden master and
+both Lichii nx2 forks have the same per-request buffers. Draft
+drafts-2026-11-11/ports-7/first-start/hle-oom/draft.v2.diff (the reviewed version: memory-wanted
+mark always taken back, the test aid's service field, one non-template Prepare), placed unchanged;
+front part draft-front.diff (eden_heap_want), placed unchanged in the same commit.
