++// when the heap refuses one. Three switches, all off unless set, all read once at the first request
++// that has an output buffer (the services start after main has applied env.txt and the game's env file):
++//
++// EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER=1: each service thread keeps the buffers its replies were built in and
++//   builds the next reply in them, instead of a new one per request. A buffer grows to the largest
++//   request its thread has seen and never shrinks; a request over EDEN_UP_HLE_REPLY_BUFFER_CAP_MIB
++//   (1 to 64, unset 4) gets a one-off buffer as before, and so does a request that comes while its
++//   thread's buffers are already in use further up the same thread (never seen; counted as "nested").
++//   Why: both warm first-start runs of 12 Nov 2026 (run1234, run1236, T130) ended at the house door
++//   with std::bad_alloc on fsp-srv, the one file-service thread, in this wrapper's buffer for one
++//   IStorage::Read of 902,912 and 480,902 bytes: the heap held about 0.9 GiB free, but in pieces, and
++//   could not grow. Off, every request with an output buffer asks the heap for a new one; on, the file
++//   thread asks only when a read is larger than any before it.
++// EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY=1: a std::bad_alloc while the wrapper reads a request's inputs and sets up its
++//   output buffers (before the service's own code runs) is caught: the buffers that were growing go,
++//   the heap gives its empty spaces back (eden_heap_trim), the caches are told memory is wanted
++//   (eden_heap_want, headless/heap_arenas.inc: the texture collector evicts at its hardest) for
++//   EDEN_UP_HLE_OOM_RETRY_MS (0 to 2000, unset 200) while this thread sleeps, and the same set-up runs
++//   once more. Refused again: the exception goes on as before (the session ends as today). Only the
++//   set-up is ever run twice: the service's code has not run, the reply has not been written, and the
++//   set-up only reads (see NOTES.md, "Why a retry cannot run anything twice").
